**Appendix B: Role Comparison**

*Digital Technology **&** Accessibility Manager vs Digital Coach*

Here I hope to be able to clarify how the Digital Technology & Accessibility Manager and Digital Coach roles are complementary but different. Both are necessary to fully realise the benefits of digital technology in education. The consultation rationale notes we would "still have digital development and technologies" - this comparison shows why pedagogical coaching capability is a separate function that technical roles do not replace.

**Role Comparison**

| **Dimension** | **Digital Technology ****&**** Accessibility Manager** | **Digital Coach** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Primary Focus** | Technical infrastructure, platforms, systems, compliance | Teaching quality improvement through digital pedagogy coaching |
| **Core Function** | Ensure digital tools work and meet accessibility standards | Help staff use digital tools effectively to improve learning |
| **Compliance Role** | WCAG compliance, accessibility audits, assistive tech procurement | Supports staff in applying accessibility principles in classroom practice |
| **Staff Interaction** | Technical training on platform features and accessibility standards | Individual coaching on pedagogical approaches, adapting to each staff member's needs |
| **Quality Alignment** | Ensures technical systems meet regulatory requirements | Supports T7/T8/T9 achievement through coaching and CPD delivery |
| **Inspection Readiness** | Technical compliance evidence | Teaching quality evidence through Learning Walks and QIP support |
| **Typical Output** | Accessible platforms, compliant systems, technical documentation | Improved teaching practice, staff confidence, learner engagement |
| **Reporting Alignment** | IT/Technical infrastructure | Quality/TLA (proposed repositioning) |

**The Key Distinction**

I believe there is an important difference between these two vital and complementary roles.

The Digital Technology & Accessibility Manager ensures we have the right tools, configured correctly, meeting compliance standards. The Digital Coach ensures staff know how to use those tools pedagogically to improve teaching and learning outcomes. This is an important part of delivering high-quality digital learning and being able to meet expectations through adaptive and inclusive practice.

*An analogy: Providing a car (technical role) is different from teaching someone to drive well (coaching role). Both are necessary for safe, effective travel.*

**Why Both Roles Are Needed**

Assistive Technology has been available within our Microsoft Ecosystem for over 10 years. While some members of staff are aware that tools such as Immersive Reader is available, few promote the use to their learners. Even fewer deliver learning in a way that enables learners to fully utilise assistive technologies for learning.

While platforms and tools are compliant and available, and while staff may become more aware of the capabilities of the assistive technology available, it is the pedagogical understanding of Digital Learning that enables learners to fully benefit from this provision, otherwise it becomes an underutilised inclusive resource.

**Both together: Accessible, compliant platforms AND staff who know how to use them to improve teaching. This is what the EEF research shows delivers the 2-3 month attainment gains - effective use, not just availability.**

The EEF summaries around Digital Learning clearly state that it is not just about the technology itself, but the pedagogy that underpins its use.

**Evidence from Staff Testimonials**

Staff testimonials consistently describe how this role supports teaching quality improvements:

*Kara Weymouth: "What sets Graeme apart is that he understands teaching first. Digital practice is a way of improving learning, not an expectation to keep up or a box to tick."*

*Jenny Gamlin: Moved from "death by PowerPoint" to interactive workshops with "increased positive feedback from learners."*

*Emily Green (TLA Manager): "This has been vital for our work as a Quality Team."*

**Conclusion**

The extended rationale provided for this restructure notes we will "still have digital development and technologies." 

Although this is true - the Digital Technology & Accessibility Manager role addresses technical infrastructure and compliance, it does not replace the pedagogical coaching function that the Digital Coach provides. 

Both capabilities are required to meet Ofsted's expectations around digital TLA and to deliver the teaching quality improvements that staff testimonials document.

We are in the middle of an interesting time particularly where FE exam boards and regulators endorse the use of AI, whereas HE does not. 

The college needs a role where they can support TLA under differing circumstances. A role where TLA experience comes first to enable the full impact of digital tools within an adaptive and fully inclusive practice. Beyond this, a strong understanding of digital pedagogy can support improved progress tracking and monitoring, enhanced engagement, and building on improved outcomes.